# MLOps Testing Rules

## Unit Tests (no MLflow, no network)
- Test feature engineering functions in isolation — input DataFrame in, output DataFrame out.
- Mock the MLflow client with `unittest.mock.patch("mlflow.log_metric")` — never call real MLflow in unit tests.
- Use `pytest.fixture` with small hardcoded DataFrames (5–20 rows). Never read from disk in unit tests.
- Evaluation metric functions must be tested against known inputs/outputs.

## Integration Tests (local MLflow tracking URI)
- Set `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI=sqlite:///tmp/test-mlruns.db` in conftest.py.
- Run the full pipeline against the committed fixture dataset (data/fixtures/train_sample.csv).
- Assert: run was created, model artifact was logged, registered model version exists.
- Clean up: delete the tmp DB in teardown.

## Serving Tests (FastAPI TestClient)
- Use `httpx.AsyncClient` with `ASGITransport` for async FastAPI serving tests.
- Load model from a tmp MLflow store seeded with a test model artifact in conftest.py.
- Assert: /health returns 200 + model name, /predict returns correct schema, /predict with bad input returns 422.

## Data Validation Tests
- Test Pandera schema against fixture data — both valid (should pass) and invalid (should raise SchemaError).
- Test distribution checks against known good/bad distributions.

## Performance Baseline Tests
- One test asserts: training on fixture data completes in < 60 seconds.
- One test asserts: serving p99 latency < 100ms on 100 sequential requests (using time.perf_counter).

## Test Markers
```python
# pytest.ini or pyproject.toml
[tool.pytest.ini_options]
markers = [
    "unit: fast, no external deps",
    "integration: requires local MLflow",
    "serving: requires model artifact",
    "slow: > 10s, skip on CI unless --run-slow",
]
```
